      "content": "Absolutely! I'll guide you through the process to ensure your schema achieves the Third Normal Form (3NF). First, review your current schema and identify all the functional dependencies. Group them by non-trivial attributes in the way they naturally occur. Look for any transitive dependencies, not just where A -> B and B -> C, but also where there are paths like A -> D -> E that could cause redundancy and update anomalies. Next, create separate relations for any groups that have partial dependencies – that is, situations where a column in a relation depends only on part of a composite primary key. Finally, ensure that each relation doesn't have transitive dependencies; no non-prime attribute should depend on any other non-prime attribute. By removing transitive dependencies and ensuring each relation is stripped down to only non-transitive attributes, your schema will be in 3NF. Once you've rightly identified and separated these dependencies, you can adjust your relations and potentially refactor the schema into smaller, coherent tables. Think about the business logic to guide the segmentation process and remember that fewer times less redundancy equates to fewer update/insertion anomalies, promoting a more efficient and logical database design.",
